# 7. In-Process Event Bus

For low latency, start with a simple single-producer/single-consumer queue abstraction. In production, you can replace this with LMAX Disruptor or Aeron.

```java
package com.trading.common.bus;

public interface EventHandler<T> {
    void onEvent(T event);
}
```

```java
package com.trading.common.bus;

import java.util.concurrent.ArrayBlockingQueue;

public class InMemoryEventBus<T> {
    private final ArrayBlockingQueue<T> queue;
    private final EventHandler<T> handler;
    private volatile boolean running = true;

    public InMemoryEventBus(int capacity, EventHandler<T> handler) {
        this.queue = new ArrayBlockingQueue<>(capacity);
        this.handler = handler;
    }

    public boolean publish(T event) {
        return queue.offer(event);
    }

    public void start() {
        Thread t = new Thread(() -> {
            while (running) {
                try {
                    T event = queue.take();
                    handler.onEvent(event);
                } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                    Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
                    break;
                } catch (Exception e) {
                    e.printStackTrace();
                }
            }
        }, "event-bus-thread");
        t.setDaemon(true);
        t.start();
    }

    public void stop() {
        running = false;
    }
}
```

This is simple, but not ideal for ultra-low latency. It is enough for an initial implementation and easy to replace later.

