# 6. Latency monitoring

We’ll use HdrHistogram for high-resolution latency tracking.

```java
package hfmsg;

import org.HdrHistogram.Histogram;

import java.util.concurrent.atomic.LongAdder;

public final class LatencyMonitor {
    private final Histogram ingressToRouteNs =
            new Histogram(3_600_000_000L, 3);
    private final Histogram endToEndNs =
            new Histogram(3_600_000_000L, 3);

    private final LongAdder routedCount = new LongAdder();
    private final LongAdder droppedCount = new LongAdder();

    public synchronized void recordIngressToRoute(long nanos) {
        ingressToRouteNs.recordValue(nanos);
    }

    public synchronized void recordEndToEnd(long nanos) {
        endToEndNs.recordValue(nanos);
    }

    public void incrementRouted() {
        routedCount.increment();
    }

    public void incrementDropped() {
        droppedCount.increment();
    }

    public long routedCount() {
        return routedCount.sum();
    }

    public long droppedCount() {
        return droppedCount.sum();
    }

    public synchronized String snapshot() {
        return String.format(
                "routed=%d dropped=%d ingress->route p50=%dns p99=%dns p99.9=%dns e2e p50=%dns p99=%dns p99.9=%dns",
                routedCount(),
                droppedCount(),
                ingressToRouteNs.getValueAtPercentile(50.0),
                ingressToRouteNs.getValueAtPercentile(99.0),
                ingressToRouteNs.getValueAtPercentile(99.9),
                endToEndNs.getValueAtPercentile(50.0),
                endToEndNs.getValueAtPercentile(99.0),
                endToEndNs.getValueAtPercentile(99.9)
        );
    }
}
```

> For extreme throughput, you’d usually use **thread-local histograms** and merge periodically instead of synchronizing on every record.

