## Memory-constrained design notes
This implementation is intentionally conservative:
- Fixed-size config struct
- Fixed-size path buffers
- No linked lists, maps, or dynamic config reload logic
- No directory scanning
- No compression
- No threads
- No heap allocations in the main loop

Approximate memory footprint is very small, mostly:
- stack buffers under a few KB
- process image from libc/runtime
- no growing memory structures

---

## Important limitation
If the application writing the log keeps the file descriptor open, then after rotation it may continue writing to the renamed file (`app.log.1`) instead of the newly created `app.log`. This is standard Unix behavior.

Common solutions:
- send the writer a signal to reopen logs
- use `copytruncate` style rotation instead of rename
- integrate with the application’s logging framework

---

