# Notes on performance

This implementation is reasonably efficient for an initial version because:

- one shared `sysinfo::System` instance is reused in the monitor loop
- sampling is periodic and bounded
- only top N processes are retained in shared state
- async Unix socket server avoids one-thread-per-client overhead
- lock contention is low:
  - monitor writes periodically
  - clients mostly read

---

# Important limitations in this initial version

For a true production-grade high-performance daemon, you would likely want to add:

- privilege separation
- authentication/authorization on the control socket
- systemd integration
- configurable sampling intervals
- cgroup/container awareness
- per-core CPU metrics
- network interface metrics
- I/O throughput and pressure metrics
