Below is a minimal working example of an embedded-style sensor-processing pipeline for accelerometer anomaly detection.

It includes:

- simulated accelerometer input
- simple preprocessing
- feature extraction
- anomaly detection with a lightweight threshold method
- a C implementation suitable for microcontrollers
- a tiny Python script to generate test data

---

## 1. Design

Pipeline:

1. Read accelerometer sample `(ax, ay, az)`
2. Compute magnitude-like signal
3. Remove gravity/baseline with a simple high-pass approach
4. Aggregate samples in a small window
5. Compute a feature: average absolute deviation
6. Flag anomaly if feature exceeds threshold

This is intentionally minimal and embedded-friendly:
- no dynamic allocation
- no floating-point-heavy ML model
- fixed-size window
- easy to port to MCU firmware

---

## 2. Minimal C implementation

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdint.h>
#include <math.h>

#define WINDOW_SIZE 32
#define ALPHA 0.95f
#define ANOMALY_THRESHOLD 1.2f

typedef struct {
    float baseline;
    float window[WINDOW_SIZE];
    uint16_t index;
    uint8_t full;
} SensorPipeline;

void pipeline_init(SensorPipeline *p) {
    p->baseline = 0.0f;
    p->index = 0;
    p->full = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < WINDOW_SIZE; i++) {
        p->window[i] = 0.0f;
    }
}

static float accel_magnitude(float ax, float ay, float az) {
    return sqrtf(ax * ax + ay * ay + az * az);
}

static float highpass_filter(SensorPipeline *p, float x) {
    p->baseline = ALPHA * p->baseline + (1.0f - ALPHA) * x;
    return x - p->baseline;
}

static void window_add(SensorPipeline *p, float value) {
    p->window[p->index++] = value;
    if (p->index >= WINDOW_SIZE) {
        p->index = 0;
        p->full = 1;
    }
}

static float compute_feature(SensorPipeline *p) {
    int count = p->full ? WINDOW_SIZE : p->index;
    if (count == 0) return 0.0f;

    float sum = 0.0f;
    for (int i = 0; i < count; i++) {
        sum += fabsf(p->window[i]);
    }
    return sum / count;
}

int pipeline_process(SensorPipeline *p, float ax, float ay, float az, float *feature_out) {
    float mag = accel_magnitude(ax, ay, az);
    float hp = highpass_filter(p, mag);
    window_add(p, hp);

    float feature = compute_feature(p);
    if (feature_out) {
        *feature_out = feature;
    }

    return (feature > ANOMALY_THRESHOLD) ? 1 : 0;
}

int main(void) {
    SensorPipeline pipeline;
    pipeline_init(&pipeline);

    // Simulated accelerometer stream:
    // mostly normal around 1g, then one burst anomaly
    float samples[][3] = {
        {0.01f, 0.02f, 0.98f},
        {0.00f, 0.01f, 1.01f},
        {0.02f, 0.01f, 0.99f},
        {0.01f, 0.00f, 1.00f},
        {0.03f, 0.02f, 1.02f},
        {0.00f, 0.01f, 0.97f},
        {0.02f, 0.02f, 1.00f},
        {0.01f, 0.01f, 0.99f},

        {0.05f, 0.04f, 1.01f},
        {0.02f, 0.03f, 0.98f},
        {0.01f, 0.02f, 1.00f},
        {0.00f, 0.01f, 1.02f},

        // anomaly burst
        {2.5f, 1.8f, 0.5f},
        {3.0f, 2.2f, 0.4f},
        {2.8f, 2.0f, 0.6f},

        {0.01f, 0.01f, 1.00f},
        {0.02f, 0.02f, 0.99f},
        {0.00f, 0.01f, 1.01f}
    };

    int n = sizeof(samples) / sizeof(samples[0]);

    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        float feature;
        int anomaly = pipeline_process(
            &pipeline,
            samples[i][0], samples[i][1], samples[i][2],
            &feature
        );

        printf("sample=%2d feature=%0.3f anomaly=%d\n", i, feature, anomaly);
    }

    return 0;
}
```

---

## 3. How it works

### Magnitude
We reduce 3-axis acceleration to one scalar:

```c
sqrt(ax^2 + ay^2 + az^2)
```

This makes the detector orientation-independent.

### High-pass filtering
A simple baseline estimate tracks slow changes like gravity:

```c
baseline = alpha * baseline + (1 - alpha) * x
hp = x - baseline
```

This highlights sudden motion.

### Windowed feature
We store recent filtered samples and compute:

```c
mean(abs(window))
```

This is a cheap measure of vibration or shock intensity.

### Threshold detector
If the feature exceeds a fixed threshold, we report anomaly.

