# Why this handles frame-rate constraints well

The important part is this pattern:

```cpp
const float fixedDt = 1.0f / 120.0f;
float accumulator = 0.0f;
```

Then each frame:

```cpp
accumulator += frameTime;
while (accumulator >= fixedDt) {
    world.Step(fixedDt);
    accumulator -= fixedDt;
}
```

This means:

- Physics always runs at a **stable fixed rate**
- Rendering can run at a different frame rate
- Simulation behaves consistently on fast and slow machines

Also:

```cpp
frameTime = std::min(frameTime, 0.25);
```

prevents the **spiral of death** if the app stalls.

And:

```cpp
if (elapsed < targetFrameTime) SDL_Delay(...)
```

caps rendering to reduce CPU usage.

