# Why this satisfies frame-rate constraints

The important part is this loop:

```cpp
accumulator += elapsed;

while (accumulator >= FIXED_DT && substeps < MAX_SUBSTEPS) {
    world.Step(FIXED_DT);
    accumulator -= FIXED_DT;
    ++substeps;
}
```

This means:

- rendering can happen at any frame rate
- physics always advances in fixed increments
- simulation remains stable and deterministic compared to variable `dt`
- `MAX_SUBSTEPS` prevents too much catch-up work in one frame
- `MAX_FRAME_TIME` clamps huge pauses

This is the standard approach for real-time simulations and games.

