# Why this works for frame-rate constraints

The important part is this section:

```js
const FIXED_DT = 1 / 60;
let accumulator = 0;

function loop() {
  const now = performance.now() / 1000;
  let frameTime = now - lastTime;
  lastTime = now;

  if (frameTime > MAX_FRAME_TIME) {
    frameTime = MAX_FRAME_TIME;
  }

  accumulator += frameTime;

  while (accumulator >= FIXED_DT) {
    world.step(FIXED_DT);
    accumulator -= FIXED_DT;
  }

  render();
  requestAnimationFrame(loop);
}
```

This ensures:

- Physics is always simulated in **equal-sized time steps**
- Simulation behaves consistently across different rendering frame rates
- Large frame spikes are clamped to avoid instability

This is much better than doing:

```js
world.step(deltaTime);
```

with a variable `deltaTime`, which can make rigid body simulations unstable.

---

