## Why this works

### 1. Fixed timestep
Physics is updated with:

```cpp
const float fixedDt = 1.0f / 120.0f;
```

This is important because rigid body simulation becomes unstable if you use a wildly varying `dt`.

---

### 2. Accumulator loop
This pattern:

```cpp
accumulator += frameTime;
while (accumulator >= fixedDt) {
    world.step(fixedDt);
    accumulator -= fixedDt;
}
```

lets rendering run at variable frame rates while physics stays deterministic and stable.

---

### 3. Frame-time clamp
This line:

```cpp
frameTime = std::min(frameTime, static_cast<double>(maxFrameTime));
```

prevents the **spiral of death** if the app stalls for a moment.

---

### 4. Semi-implicit Euler integration
This is better than explicit Euler for many real-time simulations:

```cpp
body.velocity += acceleration * dt;
body.position += body.velocity * dt;
```

It is simple and commonly used in games.

