# Why this works well for real-time simulation

## 1. Fixed timestep
Physics is updated using:

```cpp
const float fixedDt = 1.0f / 60.0f;
```

This is important because rigid body solvers are much more stable with a constant timestep than with variable frame times.

---

## 2. Accumulator pattern
The loop uses:

```cpp
accumulator += frameTime;
while (accumulator >= fixedDt) {
    world.step(fixedDt);
    accumulator -= fixedDt;
}
```

This ensures physics progresses at a stable rate even if rendering frame time fluctuates.

---

## 3. Frame-time clamping
This line prevents huge time jumps:

```cpp
float frameTime = std::min(elapsed.count(), maxFrameTime);
```

Without this, a lag spike could cause many physics steps in one frame and destabilize the simulation.

---

## 4. Impulse-based collision response
Collisions are resolved using impulses based on relative velocity, mass, and rotational inertia. This is standard for real-time rigid body engines.

---

## 5. Iterative solver
The solver runs multiple passes:

```cpp
int solverIterations = 8;
```

This improves stability for stacked or repeated contacts.

