## Why this structure works

### 1. Fixed timestep
Physics is updated at a constant rate:

```cpp
const float fixedDt = 1.0f / 120.0f;
```

This avoids instability caused by variable frame times.

---

### 2. Accumulator loop
If rendering is slower or faster than physics, the simulation still advances correctly:

```cpp
accumulator += frameTime;
while (accumulator >= fixedDt) {
    // physics step
    accumulator -= fixedDt;
}
```

---

### 3. Frame-rate cap
Rendering is limited to 60 FPS:

```cpp
if (elapsed < targetFrameTime) {
    SDL_Delay(...);
}
```

This reduces CPU usage and keeps pacing predictable.

---

### 4. Semi-implicit Euler
This is better than explicit Euler for many real-time physics cases:

```cpp
velocity += acceleration * dt;
position += velocity * dt;
```

