## Why this structure works well

### 1. Fixed timestep for physics
Physics is updated at a constant rate:

```cpp
constexpr double physicsDt = 1.0 / 120.0;
```

This avoids instability caused by variable frame times.

---

### 2. Frame-rate cap
The outer loop limits rendering/update frequency to about 60 FPS:

```cpp
constexpr double targetFrameTime = 1.0 / 60.0;
```

This prevents the simulation from consuming 100% CPU unnecessarily.

---

### 3. Accumulator pattern
This is the standard real-time simulation pattern:

```cpp
accumulator += frameTime;
while (accumulator >= physicsDt) {
    world.step(physicsDt);
    accumulator -= physicsDt;
}
```

It keeps physics deterministic and stable even if rendering fluctuates.

---

### 4. Spiral-of-death protection
This line prevents huge catch-up bursts after a pause:

```cpp
frameTime = std::min(frameTime, 0.25);
```

Without it, a lag spike could force too many physics steps in one frame.

