# Why this works

## 1. Fixed timestep
Physics is updated using:

```cpp
const float fixedDt = 1.0f / 60.0f;
```

This is important because rigid body simulation becomes unstable if you use a variable timestep directly.

---

## 2. Accumulator loop
This part decouples rendering from physics:

```cpp
accumulator += frameTime;
while (accumulator >= fixedDt) {
    world.step(fixedDt);
    accumulator -= fixedDt;
}
```

So if rendering slows down temporarily, physics still advances in fixed-size chunks.

---

## 3. Frame-time clamp
This prevents the “spiral of death”:

```cpp
frameTime = std::min(frameTime, maxFrameTime);
```

If a frame stalls badly, you avoid trying to simulate an enormous amount of physics all at once.

---

## 4. Semi-implicit Euler integration
This is more stable than explicit Euler for real-time physics:

```cpp
velocity += acceleration * dt;
position += velocity * dt;
```

