# How the frame-rate constraint works

This implementation uses a **fixed timestep**:

- Render/update frames may arrive at variable times.
- Physics always advances in chunks of `1/60` seconds.
- A frame-time accumulator stores leftover time.
- We clamp frame time with `maxFrameTime` to avoid huge jumps.
- We limit substeps per frame with `maxSubsteps` to avoid the **spiral of death**.

This is the standard real-time approach.

Core logic:

```cpp
accumulator += frameTime;
while (accumulator >= fixedDt && substeps < maxSubsteps) {
    world.step(fixedDt);
    accumulator -= fixedDt;
}
```

