## Why this is high-performance

### 1. Async I/O with Tokio
Tokio uses non-blocking I/O and a work-stealing scheduler, allowing the server to handle many concurrent connections efficiently.

### 2. Low-overhead framework
Axum is built on top of Hyper, which is fast and production-proven.

### 3. Shared state uses atomics
The request counter uses `AtomicU64`, avoiding mutex contention for a simple metric.

### 4. Multi-threaded runtime
Tokio’s multi-threaded runtime spreads work across CPU cores.

### 5. Graceful shutdown
Important in production so active requests can finish cleanly.

